A||||14||mshill||Drag racer reality check...||||9||
Z||000000||mshill||09-22-2000||01:00 AM||||Just a little note to all you guys out there racing on the street...  <P>A 40 year old man on a bicycle was killed yesterday in Roseville, CA when he was struck by a car involved in a high speed drag race in the middle of the afternoon.  Witnesses estimated the two cars were traveling in excess of 100 mph.  The 17 year old driver will definitely face criminal charges, possibly second degree murder.  His passenger may also face some charges.  The police are still looking for the second car involved in the race, and will likely face charges as well.<P>Some bad choices by some young people, when all is said and done, will likely ruin at least two families and for sure two lives.<P>I love to drive my Fiero hard, but this is kind of a wakeup call for me (even though I don't race).  I also used it as a teaching moment for my 14 year old son and explained to him how one lapse of judgment can permanently ruin a number of lives including your own.<P>Drive safe!!!||||reg||9
Z||000001||Dan86gt3.8||09-22-2000||01:38 AM||||Theese storys always pop up, but yet people still race.  I don't see this changing anytime soon.  People die all the time.  I am not saying racing is wrong or right, just that it happens.  I am not tying to sound hard, but people make bad desicions, people are at the wrong place at the wrong time, someone was riding thier bike in the right place and somebody raced in the right place for  an accident to happen.  a person died and the drivers of the cars will most likely spend the rest of thier lives behind bars.  But I don't think this will change anything...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||DjKid||09-22-2000||01:47 AM||||Yo, Death happens. I'm not saying I dont feel bad about someone getting killed. This would be the only situation about street racing where I would feel bad. If the driver of one of the two cars had died, then I wouldn't. I'm not gonna feel bad about something happening to someone due to a personal decision. Am I gonna continue to street race after reading this, probably. Just not right now, cuz my cars sitting in the backyard unable to be legally driven.! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> <P>I street race, If I get killed doing it do not feel bad for me. It was my choice.<P>------------------<BR>Ray - Blue 88 Coupe<BR><A HREF="http://blueknight.carolinafieros.com" TARGET=_blank>My Blue Knight</A> - <A HREF="http://www.carolinafieros.com" TARGET=_blank> Carolina Fieros</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Philphine||09-22-2000||01:56 AM||||i guess i got all my race jollies out while i was riding a motorcycle when i was younger (nothing better for stoplight granprix). now, as long as i can pull away from a stoplight without feeling like i'm holding up traffic and can get the occasional pressed into the seat feel weather someone's beside me or not i'm satisfied.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||GT Bastard||09-22-2000||02:27 AM||||It's a shame that a couple of punk kids had to go ruin it for everyone.  I've done more than my share of street racing, but there has rarely ever been a case where I felt an innocent bystander was endangered.  I never race in traffic, and I never race when pedestrians are nearby.  I'm not saying that it's impossible for little Jimmy to chase a ball into the street while I'm not paying attention, but if a racer keeps his/her eyes open it's highly unlikely.  The drivers know what they are risking, and should already be aware of their limits.  Bottom line:  Use common sense.  I can understand people who don't agree with street racing, but for some of us it's a primal urge that needs to be satisfied.     ||||reg||3
Z||000005||BBBAD GT||09-22-2000||09:04 AM||||These street racing threads always remind me of the Jerry Springer Show, where normally the 12-14yr olds up on the stage know they are doing something wrong, but don't care and will continue to do it.  <P>Just one note, now that really fast machines aren't black majic anymore (just call summit with a credit card and *poof*, instant 10-11 sec car), I sure hope people don't still think they can 'look out' and 'keep their eyes open' when street racing a 10-11 sec car.  The only time you have aside from trying to keeping the tires from spinning is looking at your tach/shift light to grab the next gear.  It happens very fast.<P>Are these cars out there? You better believe it and they are all over Ebay for under $10k.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/BBBAD_GT_SIG.gif">    <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||whamel||09-22-2000||09:40 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mshill:<BR><B>Just a little note to all you guys out there racing on the street...  <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks for letting us know.  It's a good reminder sometimes to get a dose of reality.  I have been hearing about places where stuff like this is common - illegal street racing with pedestrians and such getting hurt and killed all the time.  Makes me wish cops would work with us street racers and maybe set up stretches of back road or something for us to use.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">   I guess that would be 'condoning it' or something...(sigh)||||reg||1
Z||000007||80's BOY||09-22-2000||09:55 AM||||yeah, it's probably some punks going 100mph downtown or something. when we race, or see a worthy opponent and want a fair run, we go 3 miles out of town to the "strip" (read burroughsville road) where there is smooth pavement and no chance of traffic. i admit i'll screw off in town light to light, but when it comes to a close run i usually put my money where my mouth is. that's when i want a race with no interference and no arguments afterwards. what happened to those good ol' days?? ||||reg||1
Z||000008||btoth||09-22-2000||10:01 AM||||whamel-<P>i'm from OH too... ever been to Norwalk?  my friends race their muscle cars there all the time.  $5(?) for the day i think.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000009||HugGRRRFiero||09-22-2000||10:12 AM||||It's true and I agree many people are hit or killed by cars everyday, whether the car is in a street race or not.  The only thing different in the case of a street race is the fact that it probably could have been prevented.<P>I don't want to impose my beliefs, morals, or judgements on anyone, but I would hope most people would take the adrenaline rush to the dragstrip.<P>Top 10 Ten Reasons Why:<P>10. You can get an accurate timeslip you can brag about and show everyone what your car is capable of doing.<P>9. You can run with other guys in the same class, or OB which leaves the "Does he have juice?" question pretty much out of the picture.<P>8. Your family and friends can come and watch you race.<P>7. Nobody on a bike or running will jump out in front of you, unless they are really stupid.<P>6. You won't find other cars crossing your path.<P>5. You can concentrate all of your attention on shift points, tach, and what the car is doing.<P>4. If you splatter your car against the guardrail because of a mechanical failure (of course), there are medical and fire people standing by to help save you so you can race another day.<P>3. No flashing headlights and red & blue lights in your rear view mirrors.<P>2. Did I mention the adrenaline rush?  <P>1. Finally, and probably the best reason is it gives you a good excuse with your g/f, wife - b/f, or husband to spend a lot of time and money on your car.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by HugGRRRFiero (edited 09-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||whamel||09-22-2000||10:28 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by btoth:<BR><B>whamel-<P>i'm from OH too... ever been to Norwalk?  my friends race their muscle cars there all the time.  $5(?) for the day i think.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I go to Edgewater - used to race there a lot, but I"ve never been to Norwalk.  I'm still looking for a good track in Michigan though - the one by my house is tough to get on.<BR>Wade||||reg||1
Z||000011||whamel||09-22-2000||10:45 AM||||Well, not a 'street drag story', but I have first hand experience with hitting a girl on a bike a long time ago and it is one of those things that has pretty much haunted me my entire life since.  This was about 15 years ago.  Here's what happened:<P>I was driving through my neighborhood going the speed limit (I NEVER speed in school zones or residential areas EVER!) and I saw a crowd of people off on a porch to my right.  I figured there would be kids around so I slowed down and got ready to stop.  Sure enough, a young girl on a bike rode out from behind a parked van right in front of my car.  I stopped, she paniced and turned her bike INTO my car, hit it breaking the hood ornament off our Mercedes and damagine the hood and bumper.  I really KNOW this wasn't my fault, the witnesses there said it wasn't my fault, but you know what - in the eyes of the law..it was my fault and it's very hard to not blame myself for it regardless.  The girl's mother called an ambulance and berated me verbally while we waited.  The girl was taken to the hospital and had and examination and tests done.  By the way, I didn't do this out of the kindness of my own heart - at least in Kentucky when there's a vehicle/pedestrian accident the vehicle is automagically responsible for all damages to everybody.  Turns out she was fine, had nothing wrong and I had to pick up the check for the hospital, my car repairs, her bike repairs, etc. etc.<BR>Was I racing?  Heck no - I was driving very slow.  Does this still bother me to this day?  Yes, it does.  If I would have been speeding (like so many people do through my neighborhood) I probably would have killed her.  <BR>     I am a safe driver - I have been to a couple high speed driving schools, I know my limitations, and I respect others.  I do not race with other people in my car.  I do most of my high speed driving on the highway, very late at night. <P>I'm not going to get into an arguement about why I race or that it's wrong - I don't want to bump heads with anybody!  So, I'm going to just bite my tongue and not say a word about that.  I do respect your views (all of you) and I hope that you can respect mine.||||reg||1
Z||000012||BBBAD GT||09-22-2000||11:51 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by whamel:<BR><B> Makes me wish cops would work with us street racers and maybe set up stretches of back road or something for us to use. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>ummmm....thats why there are Dragstrips, nice and legal.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||stsmithgt||09-22-2000||01:12 PM||||I'm not into this downtown racing.  In Davis Ca., the most I do is see who's in front by the time your in second gear.  After that it's getting beyond stupid.  Years ago, we would gather up a race downtown then all go out to the farm land where a 1/4 mile was marked out on a road that followed (tomatoe/wheat) fields and railroad tracks.  The worst crash would be someone sliding into a ditch or getting stuck in the mud off road (so I've heard), and have to get the tractor out to get their vehicle unstuck.  If they didn't want to go out to the "track", we wouldn't race, only taunt. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  We had REAL strict police in town which helped up keep it down downtown.||||reg||6
Z||000014||Haze_Performance||09-22-2000||03:38 PM||||Just for the record:<P>On the news you hear about the poor old lady or man (90 years old) that jumped into there car and the pedal got stuck. They end up plowing into a crowd of people or through a building. Now Im not saying all old folks cant drive but the news makes you feel more upset when a young driver makes a mistake.  I myself do tend to get into a street race now and then. (NOT on side streets, busy roads...ect. But just because there are stupid kids out there with cars that are to fast for them to handle dosent mean we should damn them all. <P><BR>My 2 1/2 cents  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mycarsig.jpg">       <B>1985 Sport Coupe - Coming Soon 1987 SE</B><P><BR><B>Website's  <A HREF="http://www.WS6Fiero.com" TARGET=_blank>www.WS6Fiero.com</A> &                  <A HREF="http://www.hazeperformance.com" TARGET=_blank>www.HazePerformance.com </A></B>||||reg||14
